{{!-- The bar's one call to action, in whichever of the publication's four states applies.

The design draws a single Subscribe button, because the design has one visitor. A publication has
four, and a control that cannot act is worse than no control:

	signups open, visitor is not a member   Subscribe, into Portal's signup
	invite-only                             Sign in — new memberships are closed, so offering one lies
	already a member                        Account, into Portal's account panel
	members off entirely                    nothing; Ghost turns @site.signup_url into a feed reader
	                                        URL in that state, and a Subscribe that means RSS is bait

Ghost's own Source theme resolves the member states the same way, though it offers a separate Sign in
beside Subscribe and spells the Portal route itself — so the behaviour is what a buyer who has used
another Ghost theme expects, not a copy of it.

The open-signup href is `@site.signup_url`, not a hardcoded `#/portal/signup`: Ghost computes that
value, and a theme that spells the Portal route itself quietly overrides the platform's answer. The
other two states have no computed equivalent, so they name their Portal route.

`close()` is unconditional: every rendering of this partial sits inside the header's `siteMenu`, and
`close()` is a no-op unless the collapsed panel is open — the same thing the panel's menu links do.

Params: `class` (the button's styling, so the bar and the collapsed panel can size it differently). --}}
{{#if @site.members_enabled}}
	{{#if @member}}
		<a
			href="#/portal/account"
			data-portal="account"
			x-on:click="close()"
			class="{{class}}">{{t "Account"}}</a>
	{{else if @site.members_invite_only}}
		<a
			href="#/portal/signin"
			data-portal="signin"
			x-on:click="close()"
			class="{{class}}">{{t "Sign in"}}</a>
	{{else}}
		<a
			href="{{@site.signup_url}}"
			x-on:click="close()"
			class="{{class}}">{{t "Subscribe"}}</a>
	{{/if}}
{{/if}}
